And if so, we identify it as an MCP server.

00:38:52.176 --> 00:38:54.377
What that also means is we do things like

00:38:54.377 --> 00:38:56.976
set the minimum processes to one automatically.

00:38:57.576 --> 00:38:59.377
So what this means is there's not going to be a cold start

00:38:59.377 --> 00:39:00.377
for your users.

00:39:00.576 --> 00:39:02.377
If you're using an MCP server and you reach out

00:39:02.377 --> 00:39:04.976
and it's idle, it's going to take a few seconds,

00:39:05.377 --> 00:39:07.176
maybe more than a few seconds, 10 to 15 seconds

00:39:07.176 --> 00:39:08.976
to spin up wherever it's being hosted.

00:39:09.377 --> 00:39:11.576
By setting a minimum process, we know that means

00:39:11.576 --> 00:39:13.777
that it'll always be up and idle in the background

00:39:14.176 --> 00:39:15.777
and so that when a user wants to consume it

00:39:15.777 --> 00:39:17.976
or interact with it, they're not going to have that lag.

00:39:19.377 --> 00:39:21.576
Connect also lets you set a lot of different configurations

00:39:21.576 --> 00:39:24.176
so that if you have an MCP server that's being used by

00:39:24.576 --> 00:39:27.976
10 people at first, then 20, then 30, then 40,

00:39:28.377 --> 00:39:30.377
you can keep scaling up and decide how much compute

00:39:30.377 --> 00:39:32.377
you want to put behind it to make sure it perform

00:39:32.377 --> 00:39:33.377
it for your users.

00:39:37.576 --> 00:39:38.976
Another key thing I want to highlight,

00:39:39.176 --> 00:39:41.176
you'll see here we're looking at that feedback MCP.

00:39:41.576 --> 00:39:44.176
As I mentioned earlier, I had access in Connect

00:39:44.176 --> 00:39:45.377
and that's why I was able to connect to it.

00:39:45.377 --> 00:39:46.976
You can see my name listed right here.

00:39:47.777 --> 00:39:49.576
Well, when I want to share it with other people,

00:39:49.976 --> 00:39:53.576
I can use Connect's built-in access control list

00:39:53.777 --> 00:39:55.777
to share it with everyone in the engineering department,

00:39:55.777 --> 00:39:56.377
let's say.

00:39:56.976 --> 00:39:59.176
This can connect to your IDP that you're already using

00:39:59.176 --> 00:40:01.577
at your organization, so it just makes it very simple

00:40:01.577 --> 00:40:04.176
for you to be able to share it with those that you want to.

00:40:04.976 --> 00:40:06.776
And if someone doesn't have access to that,

00:40:06.976 --> 00:40:08.377
they're not going to be able to connect to it

00:40:08.377 --> 00:40:09.776
even if they have the URL.

00:40:10.176 --> 00:40:11.776
Connect will take a look at who has access

00:40:11.776 --> 00:40:14.176
and only those individuals will be able to use it.

00:40:18.377 --> 00:40:19.577
One of the new features we built

00:40:19.577 --> 00:40:22.176
and it was during the time as we're working on MCPs

00:40:22.176 --> 00:40:23.776
that applies to all content in Connect

00:40:24.176 --> 00:40:25.377
is a usage dashboard.

00:40:25.976 --> 00:40:27.377
If I've published an MCP,

00:40:27.577 --> 00:40:28.976
something that I'm probably going to be interested is

00:40:29.176 --> 00:40:30.377
how many people are using it?

00:40:31.176 --> 00:40:33.776
That can be a great proxy for is it valuable to people?

00:40:33.776 --> 00:40:35.577
Like, when are they using it?

00:40:35.976 --> 00:40:38.776
Do I need to take a look at my settings

00:40:38.776 --> 00:40:40.176
and increase the amount of compute behind it

00:40:40.176 --> 00:40:41.176
to make sure it's up and running?

00:40:41.776 --> 00:40:43.776
Out of the box within Connect, as Neil mentioned,

00:40:43.776 --> 00:40:45.377
you're able to see this usage pattern.

00:40:45.377 --> 00:40:47.377
You don't have to capture that yourself

00:40:47.377 --> 00:40:48.377
and then go back and look at it.

00:40:48.577 --> 00:40:50.776
It's built in so you can quickly take a look and see

00:40:50.776 --> 00:40:52.776
and determine how many people are using it.

00:40:55.976 --> 00:40:57.577
And lastly, this is a recent feature

00:40:57.577 --> 00:40:58.976
and one that I'm really excited about.

00:40:59.176 --> 00:41:02.776
For admins, we have this clients tab in our system page.

00:41:03.377 --> 00:41:06.176
What this allows you to do is see the AI clients

00:41:06.176 --> 00:41:09.377
that have connected to Connect and to the MCP service.

00:41:09.976 --> 00:41:11.577
So what this means is you have better insight

00:41:11.577 --> 00:41:12.377
and as you're using them,

00:41:12.377 --> 00:41:13.776
if you need to revoke them, you can

00:41:13.776 --> 00:41:15.776
and it just gives you that UI in one place

00:41:15.776 --> 00:41:16.776
where you can take a look.

00:41:17.776 --> 00:41:20.176
With the exception of this clients tab,

00:41:20.377 --> 00:41:22.377
everything else I showed like the usage dashboard

00:41:22.377 --> 00:41:23.976
and the settings, that's also features

00:41:23.976 --> 00:41:25.776
that applies to all content on Connect,

00:41:25.776 --> 00:41:27.176
not just MCP servers.

00:41:30.577 --> 00:41:32.176
So that's a quick overview of how you can manage

00:41:32.176 --> 00:41:34.577
and how you can authenticate to MCP servers on Connect.

00:41:35.377 --> 00:41:36.976
I appreciate everyone sitting through that

00:41:36.976 --> 00:41:37.976
and asking questions.

00:41:38.377 --> 00:41:40.577
At this point, we're going to transition to Q&A.

00:41:41.577 --> 00:41:44.176
Neil, I'm going to go take a look at some of the questions coming.

00:41:44.176 --> 00:41:45.976
I saw some, but I'll start with one that I saw

00:41:45.976 --> 00:41:48.976
that I touched on the authentication piece

00:41:48.976 --> 00:41:51.577
where, you know, you can use a .env file

00:41:51.577 --> 00:41:52.976
to store an API key,

00:41:52.976 --> 00:41:56.377
but the OAuth browser flow is one

00:41:56.377 --> 00:41:57.976
that is a little bit more secure.

00:41:58.377 --> 00:41:59.377
I kind of just touched on, like I said,

00:41:59.377 --> 00:42:00.377
could you go a little more detail

00:42:00.377 --> 00:42:02.377
about what that actually is doing under the hood

00:42:02.377 --> 00:42:03.377
and why it's so important?

00:42:03.377 --> 00:42:04.776
And then we'll move on to the Q&A.

00:42:04.776 --> 00:42:06.976
So I'm going to go ahead and start with the first question.

00:42:06.976 --> 00:42:09.377
Neil, I'm going to go take a look at some of the questions

00:42:10.176 --> 00:42:11.176
and why it's so important.

00:42:12.176 --> 00:42:13.176
Yeah, for sure.

00:42:15.176 --> 00:42:20.377
So the alternative, there are a couple alternatives, right?

00:42:20.377 --> 00:42:24.577
So one alternative is that you don't have any authentication

00:42:24.577 --> 00:42:31.377
on your MCP server and anyone can access it.

00:42:31.577 --> 00:42:32.377
We probably don't want that.

00:42:32.377 --> 00:42:33.577
That's not really what we're talking about.

00:42:33.577 --> 00:42:36.377
That's not what we're talking about with Connect for sure.

00:42:36.976 --> 00:42:38.577
So if we're assuming that you need,

00:42:38.577 --> 00:42:40.976
that someone should be able to, should have to authenticate,

00:42:41.176 --> 00:42:46.577
you've got two options that really work for AI clients

00:42:46.577 --> 00:42:48.976
for how MCP is set up.

00:42:49.776 --> 00:42:52.776
The .env file that Matt mentioned,

00:42:52.776 --> 00:42:53.976
that's kind of how you do it

00:42:53.976 --> 00:42:57.776
if you're in an interactive like R or Python session.

00:42:57.976 --> 00:42:59.976
I guess the .env is more of a Python thing.

00:43:01.377 --> 00:43:05.976
But, and so you would have your API key

00:43:05.976 --> 00:43:08.377
in plain text basically locally.

00:43:09.577 --> 00:43:13.776
With MCP clients, they do it a little bit differently.

00:43:13.776 --> 00:43:17.577
You have to specify the API key somewhere else.

00:43:17.976 --> 00:43:22.377
And so you basically, when you do the connection,

00:43:22.377 --> 00:43:26.577
Matt showed how you can, most under the hood,

00:43:26.577 --> 00:43:29.577
I think all of the AI applications I know

00:43:29.577 --> 00:43:32.976
have some JSON thing that has their configuration in it.

00:43:34.176 --> 00:43:37.377
There is, Cloud has a CLI codex, I think there's two,

00:43:37.377 --> 00:43:38.377
where you can add things.

00:43:38.377 --> 00:43:41.377
Basically you specify, add an extra HTTP header

00:43:41.377 --> 00:43:44.976
that is the authorization header that includes my API key.

00:43:45.377 --> 00:43:46.776
And so the effect is the same,

00:43:46.776 --> 00:43:52.176
that you've got your API key in plain text in a file somewhere.

00:43:53.776 --> 00:43:58.976
The good news for the AI security side of things

00:43:58.976 --> 00:44:02.577
is that it's not immediately in the model's context, right?

00:44:02.577 --> 00:44:05.776
So at that point, all the model sees is,

00:44:05.776 --> 00:44:07.776
here's an MCP server that has these tools.

00:44:07.776 --> 00:44:08.976
I can call those tools.

00:44:08.976 --> 00:44:15.976
It doesn't see how the MCP server handles its HTTP requests.

00:44:16.976 --> 00:44:19.776
But it is still in plain text on a file.

00:44:19.976 --> 00:44:23.776
The other thing is that API keys are long lived.

00:44:25.176 --> 00:44:29.976
I think historically in Connect, they have not had expiration.

00:44:31.377 --> 00:44:34.976
And so, and they have access, they're not scoped either.

00:44:35.577 --> 00:44:40.176
I can downscope it to only have viewing access at best,

00:44:40.176 --> 00:44:41.776
but it can view all of my content.

00:44:42.776 --> 00:44:44.976
But otherwise, the kind of default is,

00:44:44.976 --> 00:44:46.976
this is an API key that could do anything I could do

00:44:46.976 --> 00:44:47.976
if I were logged in.

00:44:48.577 --> 00:44:55.176
And that's not something that I love having floating around places,

00:44:55.176 --> 00:44:59.377
particularly when you see stories about AI agents

00:44:59.377 --> 00:45:01.776
that will go read different directories

00:45:01.776 --> 00:45:02.976
that maybe you didn't ask it to.

00:45:05.176 --> 00:45:08.176
So the OAuth flow handles it differently

00:45:08.976 --> 00:45:14.577
and deals in short-lived tokens that can get refreshed.

00:45:14.976 --> 00:45:17.776
And without getting into the specific mechanics

00:45:17.776 --> 00:45:19.776
of how it works, the important thing is that,

00:45:20.377 --> 00:45:23.176
for one, you're not pulling a long-lived API key

00:45:23.776 --> 00:45:26.377
and keeping that in plain text accessible.

00:45:29.176 --> 00:45:31.176
And I think that's the big one,

00:45:31.176 --> 00:45:32.776
is that you don't have to deal with that.

00:45:33.776 --> 00:45:35.776
There's another OAuth-related point, though,

00:45:35.776 --> 00:45:39.976
that I also want to call out that Matt alluded to,

00:45:40.577 --> 00:45:44.176
and I failed to mention it in my part as well.

00:45:45.776 --> 00:45:48.577
As with other things that you build on Connect,

00:45:48.577 --> 00:45:51.976
you can leverage the third-party integrations feature

00:45:51.976 --> 00:45:53.176
that Connect has.

00:45:56.377 --> 00:46:00.976
And this allows you to either do viewer-based integrations

00:46:01.176 --> 00:46:07.176
where the person that is using the MCP server,

00:46:08.176 --> 00:46:10.577
you're delegating to their credentials

00:46:10.577 --> 00:46:12.976
to access resources that they have access to,

00:46:12.976 --> 00:46:16.377
whether that's in Snowflake or Databricks or what have you,

00:46:17.176 --> 00:46:19.577
or whether they're more of a service account model.

00:46:20.577 --> 00:46:24.176
And this was actually very important for me

00:46:24.176 --> 00:46:28.377
in the Zendesk MCP server example that I mentioned

00:46:29.176 --> 00:46:35.176
because Zendesk, at least at the time,

00:46:35.176 --> 00:46:36.976
the only API keys that they would give

00:46:36.976 --> 00:46:39.377
would be for full admin access.

00:46:40.176 --> 00:46:44.176
So if I wanted to build something

00:46:44.176 --> 00:46:46.776
that went straight to Zendesk,

00:46:47.577 --> 00:46:50.976
I would have to not only get an API token

00:46:50.976 --> 00:46:53.776
that had access to read and delete and write

00:46:53.776 --> 00:46:55.377
and do everything,

00:46:55.976 --> 00:46:58.577
I would also have to share that with everybody on the team

00:46:58.577 --> 00:46:59.776
who wanted to use this.

00:47:01.176 --> 00:47:02.176
That's not good.

00:47:03.976 --> 00:47:05.377
And I was not allowed to do that.

00:47:06.377 --> 00:47:09.577
So that kind of ruled out having a CLI and other things

00:47:09.577 --> 00:47:11.976
where I could pass in credentials in that way.

00:47:12.976 --> 00:47:15.776
And so what I ended up doing

00:47:15.776 --> 00:47:18.176
is setting up an OAuth integration,

00:47:18.176 --> 00:47:19.976
different sense of OAuth here,

00:47:19.976 --> 00:47:22.377
but an integration between Connect and Zendesk

00:47:22.377 --> 00:47:24.377
through an OAuth application on Zendesk

00:47:24.776 --> 00:47:28.176
that would do a similar short-lived token exchange

00:47:28.176 --> 00:47:29.776
with a service account model.

00:47:30.176 --> 00:47:34.377
And so not only did I remove the need

00:47:34.377 --> 00:47:38.776
to distribute this broad API key of Zendesk,

00:47:40.176 --> 00:47:42.377
I also eliminated the need to have that at all

00:47:42.377 --> 00:47:44.976
because of the way that Connect and Zendesk could integrate.

00:47:45.577 --> 00:47:49.776
So in the end, people on my team,

00:47:49.776 --> 00:47:51.577
on the support team, elsewhere,

00:47:51.776 --> 00:47:55.176
can access this MCP server

00:47:55.577 --> 00:47:58.776
and not have to pull API keys from anything.

00:47:58.976 --> 00:48:01.176
And I have control over who has access

00:48:01.176 --> 00:48:04.976
because I manage that through Connect.

00:48:06.176 --> 00:48:09.176
So there's a couple of layers of authentication,

00:48:09.176 --> 00:48:11.577
authorization that happen there

00:48:11.577 --> 00:48:14.377
that Connect largely just handles for you

00:48:14.377 --> 00:48:17.776
that really help with your access control and governance.

00:48:20.377 --> 00:48:21.377
Thanks, Neil.

00:48:22.377 --> 00:48:24.377
I have a comment that actually leads into our next question

00:48:24.377 --> 00:48:26.776
because I had previously worked at an organization

00:48:26.776 --> 00:48:28.177
where we had a shared API key

00:48:28.177 --> 00:48:29.777
for something across three different teams.

00:48:29.777 --> 00:48:31.377
And then you knew if you did something wrong,

00:48:31.377 --> 00:48:32.777
you had to then email everyone saying,

00:48:32.777 --> 00:48:35.576
I'm sorry, we need to get a new API key and redistribute it.

00:48:36.177 --> 00:48:37.976
And then actually, we have two questions

00:48:37.976 --> 00:48:39.777
about distribution of skills.

00:48:39.777 --> 00:48:42.777
So someone had a comment that I saw on my screen,

00:48:42.777 --> 00:48:44.177
the Connect Skills MCP.

00:48:44.177 --> 00:48:45.177
And the question were,

00:48:45.177 --> 00:48:47.377
are MCPs a useful way to roll out

00:48:47.377 --> 00:48:49.177
consistent skills organization-wide?

00:48:49.576 --> 00:48:50.777
And then also somebody asked about,

00:48:50.777 --> 00:48:54.177
what are your thoughts on distributing shared skills via MCP?

00:48:54.177 --> 00:48:55.177
So two related ones.

00:48:55.177 --> 00:48:57.177
I'll take a quick answer since I had that up there

00:48:57.177 --> 00:48:58.576
and then Neil, turn it over to you.

00:48:58.777 --> 00:49:03.377
But I think one of the very powerful things about MCP

00:49:03.976 --> 00:49:05.976
is that it's such a well-adopted standard

00:49:05.976 --> 00:49:07.576
versus skills right now.

00:49:07.777 --> 00:49:09.777
It is less clear to me about a,

00:49:09.777 --> 00:49:11.377
hey, this is the distribution standard

00:49:11.377 --> 00:49:13.177
that people seem to be gravitating towards

00:49:13.177 --> 00:49:14.177
across the industry.

00:49:14.177 --> 00:49:17.177
So what you saw on my screen was I was exploring

00:49:17.177 --> 00:49:19.576
if you could use MCP as a skill distribution.

00:49:19.976 --> 00:49:20.976
I believe you can,

00:49:21.576 --> 00:49:24.377
but I don't think that it is necessarily the best pattern

00:49:24.377 --> 00:49:26.377
and I don't think it's necessarily one

00:49:26.377 --> 00:49:30.177
that the software community has kind of coalesced behind.

00:49:30.177 --> 00:49:31.177
Like, oh, let's do it this way.

00:49:31.177 --> 00:49:32.976
But Neil, you would know much better to me.

00:49:32.976 --> 00:49:34.576
So I'll toss that question over to you

00:49:34.576 --> 00:49:35.976
about distribution of skills.

00:49:36.177 --> 00:49:37.976
Yeah, I think that's right.

00:49:37.976 --> 00:49:40.377
I think that you can.

00:49:40.576 --> 00:49:44.976
I mean, ultimately, we're dealing in APIs that return text.

00:49:45.177 --> 00:49:48.576
And so you can.

00:49:48.576 --> 00:49:53.777
It is certainly not the primary value proposition of MCP

00:49:54.177 --> 00:49:57.576
to just be a kind of static repository of skills.

00:49:58.976 --> 00:50:02.777
You could distribute skills as static content on Connect,

00:50:02.777 --> 00:50:04.177
Connect to a static content.

00:50:04.377 --> 00:50:08.177
I believe Posit Package Manager is building out.

00:50:08.576 --> 00:50:09.976
I'm not sure what the exact status that is,

00:50:09.976 --> 00:50:11.177
but I believe they're working on stuff

00:50:11.177 --> 00:50:15.177
around skill distribution as a similar type of artifact

00:50:15.177 --> 00:50:17.177
to other packages and things

00:50:17.177 --> 00:50:19.576
that they do traditionally manage.

00:50:20.777 --> 00:50:25.777
One thing that I have tried,

00:50:25.976 --> 00:50:28.076
and this is just my own personal,

00:50:28.076 --> 00:50:29.576
because we're all in this trend

00:50:29.576 --> 00:50:31.476
trying to like try different things

00:50:31.476 --> 00:50:34.877
and see what works with the technology today.

00:50:35.476 --> 00:50:36.877
But I was working on an MCP server

00:50:36.877 --> 00:50:39.476
and it really needed a skill to explain.

00:50:39.476 --> 00:50:40.576
First, you call this tool,

00:50:40.576 --> 00:50:42.277
then you call this if you want to do this thing.

00:50:42.777 --> 00:50:46.677
And so I wrote a skill to go with it.

00:50:46.677 --> 00:50:48.576
And I was like, well, now I've got a distribution problem.

00:50:48.576 --> 00:50:50.777
Do I need a plug-in marketplace

00:50:50.777 --> 00:50:51.976
or some kind of thing like this?

00:50:51.976 --> 00:50:59.877
And so what I did is in the description of the MCP server,

00:51:00.677 --> 00:51:04.576
which comes across the thing, it says use this skill.

00:51:04.576 --> 00:51:06.377
And if you don't have this skill,

00:51:06.476 --> 00:51:09.976
call this MCP endpoint and it will give you this skill.

00:51:10.877 --> 00:51:13.777
And so the effect is you get the distribution

00:51:13.777 --> 00:51:15.976
of the MCP server by URL

00:51:16.076 --> 00:51:20.277
and it's able to bootstrap itself with the skill.

00:51:20.677 --> 00:51:22.277
If it already had the skill by other means,

00:51:22.277 --> 00:51:23.677
it wouldn't have to call that.

00:51:23.877 --> 00:51:25.576
And that has seemed to work.

00:51:25.877 --> 00:51:28.677
I'm not saying I'm recommending that as best practice,

00:51:28.677 --> 00:51:29.777
but it was more of a like,

00:51:29.777 --> 00:51:33.076
how do I distribute skills and MCP together?

00:51:34.377 --> 00:51:36.076
And it kind of works.

00:51:36.076 --> 00:51:37.777
So your mileage may vary.

00:51:40.677 --> 00:51:42.177
I'm going to couple these next two questions

00:51:42.177 --> 00:51:44.277
because they're related, asking about what version

00:51:44.277 --> 00:51:46.476
of Posit Connect you need for these features

00:51:46.476 --> 00:51:48.976
and also online references.

00:51:49.877 --> 00:51:52.377
We do have a, in our documentation,

00:51:52.377 --> 00:51:54.476
we have a page all about MCP servers

00:51:54.476 --> 00:51:56.677
that goes over the authentication,

00:51:57.677 --> 00:51:58.476
how you connect to them

00:51:58.476 --> 00:52:00.076
and gives you a nice overview of that.

00:52:00.377 --> 00:52:03.976
And then in terms of what version of Posit Connect you need,

00:52:04.177 --> 00:52:06.777
gosh, we've rolled out support for MCP gradually

00:52:06.777 --> 00:52:08.877
since about February, March timeframe.

00:52:09.177 --> 00:52:11.677
The clients tab that I showed that I'm a big fan of,

00:52:11.677 --> 00:52:13.377
I think, gives admins the ability to see

00:52:13.377 --> 00:52:15.177
who has authenticated to their server.

00:52:15.377 --> 00:52:17.277
That just came out in the latest release in June.

00:52:17.277 --> 00:52:19.976
So over, if Neil correct, from February

00:52:19.976 --> 00:52:22.677
to just the June release,

00:52:22.677 --> 00:52:25.377
we've been adding more of these features that we showed today.

00:52:25.777 --> 00:52:28.076
Yes, that's true.

00:52:30.076 --> 00:52:32.377
You can still run MCP servers

00:52:32.377 --> 00:52:36.177
on older versions of Connect than that though.

00:52:36.277 --> 00:52:38.377
You just won't get some of the nice features

00:52:38.377 --> 00:52:40.076
that Matt showed.

00:52:40.677 --> 00:52:44.677
But ultimately, if you're using Fast MCP,

00:52:44.677 --> 00:52:48.076
that's based on the same kind of Fast API,

00:52:48.076 --> 00:52:51.976
Python hosting that Connect has done before.

00:52:52.576 --> 00:52:55.076
So you should be able to do it.

00:52:55.076 --> 00:52:56.976
There may be a few,

00:52:57.377 --> 00:53:01.677
there's certainly some features that we have added.

00:53:01.677 --> 00:53:03.476
There's certainly enhancements to how it works

00:53:03.476 --> 00:53:07.076
with these third-party integrations that is new.

00:53:07.177 --> 00:53:10.377
The OAuth flow is new as of,

00:53:10.377 --> 00:53:12.476
I want to say December or January.

00:53:13.677 --> 00:53:16.277
But like if you're on Connect from two years ago,

00:53:16.377 --> 00:53:20.377
you could technically deploy Fast MCP.

00:53:20.677 --> 00:53:23.076
It would show up as a Python API.

00:53:23.277 --> 00:53:25.877
You would have to use an API key to authenticate,

00:53:26.476 --> 00:53:27.877
but it would probably work

00:53:27.877 --> 00:53:30.177
just without a lot of these conveniences here.

00:53:31.476 --> 00:53:31.976
Great call out.

00:53:31.976 --> 00:53:33.076
Thanks for calling that out, Neil.

00:53:33.076 --> 00:53:33.777
We had a follow-up.

00:53:33.777 --> 00:53:35.576
So yes, you could have used it before.

00:53:35.877 --> 00:53:37.976
If you upgrade, it won't auto-detect.

00:53:37.976 --> 00:53:39.976
We only auto-detect on first deployment,

00:53:39.976 --> 00:53:42.277
but you can go in and check and designate something

00:53:42.277 --> 00:53:45.277
to show up as an MCP server or uncheck it

00:53:45.277 --> 00:53:45.976
if for some reason you say,

00:53:45.976 --> 00:53:46.877
I don't want to show up there.

00:53:46.877 --> 00:53:48.677
So great call, Neil,

00:53:48.677 --> 00:53:50.277
that you could have been using it before that,

00:53:50.476 --> 00:53:52.677
but some of the more recent updates

00:53:52.677 --> 00:53:55.277
or versions of Connect have some of those nice tabs.

00:53:57.976 --> 00:53:59.377
We have a few questions from people

00:53:59.377 --> 00:54:01.177
who gave their names with specific issues.

00:54:01.177 --> 00:54:03.076
So we'll try to follow up with you separately there.

00:54:03.076 --> 00:54:04.277
But looking at the time,

00:54:04.576 --> 00:54:06.177
I think we have time for one more question.

00:54:06.177 --> 00:54:07.476
I think this is a good one, Neil.

00:54:09.277 --> 00:54:11.076
You mentioned the GitHub MCP

00:54:11.277 --> 00:54:13.377
and just that it has a whole lot of tools.

00:54:13.976 --> 00:54:15.177
This is kind of a broad question,

00:54:15.177 --> 00:54:17.277
but do you have, with what you've explored,

00:54:17.277 --> 00:54:19.476
some sort of heuristic or intuition

00:54:19.476 --> 00:54:21.677
of how many tools you should put in?

00:54:21.677 --> 00:54:23.576
Like when does an MCP come too large

00:54:24.177 --> 00:54:25.877
in terms of how you approach building them

00:54:25.877 --> 00:54:26.976
and thinking about MCPs?

00:54:27.777 --> 00:54:31.777
You can always do an evaluation if you want.

00:54:31.777 --> 00:54:33.677
And there's a few answers here, though.

00:54:33.677 --> 00:54:39.677
One is provide your model with the tools that it needs

00:54:39.677 --> 00:54:41.177
and nothing more.

00:54:41.377 --> 00:54:43.677
And if you have a different need

00:54:43.677 --> 00:54:45.476
for a different subset of tools,

00:54:45.476 --> 00:54:47.277
then make a different one

00:54:47.976 --> 00:54:50.677
since it is relatively small.

00:54:50.677 --> 00:54:51.677
And if you don't,

00:54:51.677 --> 00:54:53.177
then you don't have to worry about it.

00:54:53.177 --> 00:54:53.976
And if you do,

00:54:53.976 --> 00:54:55.377
then you don't have to worry about it.

00:54:55.677 --> 00:54:58.677
It is relatively much cheaper these days

00:54:58.677 --> 00:55:02.476
for us to build applications, build APIs.

00:55:03.677 --> 00:55:08.076
You can have more bespoke MCP servers

00:55:08.677 --> 00:55:10.277
for different use cases.

00:55:12.277 --> 00:55:14.877
Interestingly, one of the things

00:55:14.877 --> 00:55:16.476
that the GitHub MCP does now

00:55:16.476 --> 00:55:21.076
is that you can include request headers

00:55:21.076 --> 00:55:24.277
or different URL path segments,

00:55:24.476 --> 00:55:27.177
and it will only select certain tools.

00:55:27.177 --> 00:55:27.777
So you can say,

00:55:27.777 --> 00:55:30.777
I only want the tools that have to do with issues.

00:55:30.777 --> 00:55:31.277
And in fact,

00:55:31.277 --> 00:55:33.777
I only want the read-only tools to do with issues.

00:55:33.777 --> 00:55:35.976
And there is a URL that you can take for that.

00:55:35.976 --> 00:55:37.576
So there's some cleverness that you could do there

00:55:37.576 --> 00:55:40.177
if you wanted to have different subsets of tools

00:55:40.177 --> 00:55:44.377
but kind of hosting them out of the same base application.

00:55:44.877 --> 00:55:46.377
I will also note that

00:55:48.576 --> 00:55:51.277
there's been a lot of movement on this.

00:55:51.277 --> 00:55:53.976
I mean, obviously the field is moving pretty fast anyway,

00:55:53.976 --> 00:55:57.777
but since the initial MCP came out,

00:55:57.777 --> 00:56:00.076
wow, that's a lot of tokens we're burning.

00:56:00.677 --> 00:56:02.177
Cloud Code, for instance,

00:56:02.177 --> 00:56:05.277
has added what's called a tool search capability.

00:56:05.277 --> 00:56:07.177
And so my understanding of how it works

00:56:07.177 --> 00:56:12.877
is that if an MCP server has more

00:56:12.877 --> 00:56:14.976
than some threshold number of tools

00:56:14.976 --> 00:56:17.177
or tokens consumed by the thing,

00:56:17.576 --> 00:56:19.777
it doesn't load them into context

00:56:19.976 --> 00:56:23.777
and it dynamically, you call for tools,

00:56:23.976 --> 00:56:26.677
you say, I want to do something with GitHub MCP or whatever.

00:56:26.877 --> 00:56:31.576
It first calls a local tool search to look up within those

00:56:31.576 --> 00:56:34.076
and then selectively pulls them in as you need them.

00:56:34.476 --> 00:56:36.476
So some of these things,

00:56:37.476 --> 00:56:42.277
the agent harnesses have done some things to manage it as well.

00:56:43.476 --> 00:56:48.476
But in general, I think when you're designing an MCP server,

00:56:49.076 --> 00:56:51.277
I think kind of think backwards of that

00:56:51.277 --> 00:56:53.476
from how you might design like a REST API

00:56:53.476 --> 00:56:54.476
where you're like, all right,

00:56:54.476 --> 00:56:56.377
this is all of the information in my system.

00:56:56.377 --> 00:56:58.177
I'm just going to put a basic, you know,

00:56:58.476 --> 00:57:01.377
get and post API around the records here

00:57:01.377 --> 00:57:03.777
so I can do CRUD type operations.

00:57:03.976 --> 00:57:05.177
Let's just get everything out there

00:57:05.177 --> 00:57:08.177
and then you choose what to use or is useful or not.

00:57:08.476 --> 00:57:10.177
I think with MCP, you want to do it the other way.

00:57:10.177 --> 00:57:11.576
It's like, I need another tool.

00:57:11.576 --> 00:57:13.076
All right, I'm going to add another tool,

00:57:13.177 --> 00:57:14.976
but don't add it until you actually need it.

00:57:15.476 --> 00:57:16.476
No, thanks.

00:57:16.476 --> 00:57:17.976
I definitely think that's a great way to think about it.

00:57:17.976 --> 00:57:20.677
It's just an API, like you said, just made for AI clients.

00:57:20.677 --> 00:57:21.677
And then you're right.

00:57:21.677 --> 00:57:23.177
I think when MCP first came out,

00:57:23.177 --> 00:57:25.076
we were probably talking 32,000,

00:57:25.076 --> 00:57:27.476
maybe 100,000 token context windows.

00:57:27.476 --> 00:57:28.377
A lot has changed since then.

00:57:28.377 --> 00:57:29.576
Now we're at 200,000.

00:57:29.576 --> 00:57:30.576
It feels like it's pretty standard.

00:57:30.576 --> 00:57:31.576
So pretty different.

00:57:32.076 --> 00:57:32.576
Yeah.

00:57:32.576 --> 00:57:36.377
I mean, you still don't want to burn tokens on noise.

00:57:36.377 --> 00:57:37.677
Like if you don't need,

00:57:37.976 --> 00:57:40.277
if you're not going to call all 100 of those tools,

00:57:40.277 --> 00:57:43.677
like that's just like, you know, saying,

00:57:44.177 --> 00:57:45.976
I want you to answer this question,

00:57:45.976 --> 00:57:49.177
but I first want you to go read the A section in the phone book.

00:57:49.177 --> 00:57:50.677
You know, like it just doesn't help.

00:57:50.677 --> 00:57:51.177
Yeah.

00:57:51.177 --> 00:57:53.177
And so if you can avoid it, avoid it.

00:57:53.976 --> 00:57:54.677
Absolutely.

00:57:55.576 --> 00:57:56.777
Well, we are right at time.

00:57:56.777 --> 00:57:59.177
So want to say thank you to everyone for coming out.

00:57:59.476 --> 00:58:00.277
Was put in the chat,

00:58:00.277 --> 00:58:02.777
but we have had some blog posts on MCP recently.

00:58:02.777 --> 00:58:04.277
If you want to look for more material,

00:58:04.476 --> 00:58:06.076
we have docs on Positive Connect.

00:58:06.576 --> 00:58:07.976
We'd love any feedback you have.

00:58:07.976 --> 00:58:10.177
We're continuing to work to make Connect just a great place

00:58:10.177 --> 00:58:11.377
to host your MCP servers.

00:58:11.377 --> 00:58:12.976
So feel free to provide any feedback.

00:58:13.377 --> 00:58:15.377
Thank you again for taking the time to talk with us today.

00:58:15.576 --> 00:58:16.076
Thank you, Neil.

00:58:17.177 --> 00:58:17.677
Thank you.

00:58:19.877 --> 00:58:20.377
Bye everyone.
